Q: Detect whether PowerShell scripts can be executed from Windows Batch A: <h2>Constructing the logic in PowerShell</h2>
<p>If we can do it inside a PowerShell window, we can do this:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>if ((Get-ExecutionPolicy) -eq 'RemoteSigned') {
    C:\Users\username\file.ps1
} else {
    Write-Host 'PowerShell scripts have not been enabled.  Please run Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned to enable them, then try again.'
}
</code></pre>
<p>But sometimes the path to the script or the script fie name could contain white spaces. It would be better off to quote it as string and invoked by
the <em>call operator</em> <code>&amp;</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>if ((Get-ExecutionPolicy) -eq 'RemoteSigned') {
    &amp; 'C:\Users\username\file.ps1'
} else {
    Write-Host 'PowerShell scripts have not been enabled.  Please run Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned to enable them, then try again.'
}
</code></pre>
<p>Reference: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_operators?view=powershell-7.6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">about_Opeators - PowerShell | Microsoft Learn</a></p>
<h2>Use PowerShell in VS post-build event</h2>
<p>In Visual Studio post-build event box it accepts batch commands only. Thus we need to use the <code>powershell -Command &quot;...&quot;</code> like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>powershell -Command &quot;
    if ((Get-ExecutionPolicy) -eq 'RemoteSigned') {
        &amp; 'C:\Users\username\file.ps1'
    } else {
        Write-Host 'PowerShell scripts have not been enabled.  Please run Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned to enable them, then try again.'
    }
&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>But unfortunately the batch interpreter cannot handle a string across multiple lines, we just need to merge those lines into this one-liner:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>powershell -Command &quot;if ((Get-ExecutionPolicy) -eq 'RemoteSigned') { &amp; 'C:\Users\username\file.ps1' } else { Write-Host 'PowerShell scripts have not been enabled.  Please run Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned to enable them, then try again.' }&quot;
</code></pre>
